WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.960
Campus lunch should be simple, but kitchens
often prepare before they

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:05.700
know where people will eat.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.680
Mealers helps, one caterer, coordinate
choices across nearby campus

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.600
kitchens before cooking begins.

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:17.300
A diner checks the meal, walking distance,
and declared allergens, then

00:00:17.300 --> 00:00:18.660
confirms a suitable option.

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.360
At pickup, staff validate the booking code.

00:00:21.860 --> 00:00:25.440
Kitchen managers see committed demand,
protect a walk-in buffer, and

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:26.800
approve preparation quantities.

00:00:26.800 --> 00:00:30.260
They record collection and leftovers
separately.

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:31.260
Who pays?

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:34.620
Our proposed customer is the campus caterer.

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:37.760
The business model is a monthly subscription
per kitchen.

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:39.540
Priced in rupees.

00:00:40.020 --> 00:00:41.680
The price still needs validation.

00:00:42.300 --> 00:00:45.240
Forecasting and pre-order tools already
exist.

00:00:45.720 --> 00:00:48.960
Our focus is voluntary coordination between
nearby kitchens.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.320
A pilot must show whether that creates useful
value after software

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.100
costs and extra staff effort.

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:58.880
We would start with two kitchens, comparing
leftovers,

00:00:59.720 --> 00:01:02.480
shortages, and pickup completion against a
baseline.

00:01:03.080 --> 00:01:04.980
Mealers is a synthetic MVP,

00:01:05.540 --> 00:01:08.640
customers, revenue, and savings remain
unproven.

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:10.560
The next step is an operator pilot.
